## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a streaming, per-tenant UI with time-based playback, memory controls, and precise cleanup when tenants switch. It exercises useSyncExternalStore, memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow), bounded ring buffers to cap memory, React.memo for EventRow components, playback controls (play/pause and seek), and tenant switch cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- useSyncExternalStore for external streams
- memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
- Bounded ring buffer to cap memory
- React.memo for EventRow components
- Playback controls: play/pause and seek
- Tenant switch cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Stream wrapper using useSyncExternalStore
function useAuditStream(tenantId) {
  const storeRef = useRef(null);
  if (!storeRef.current) {
    storeRef.current = createAuditStore(tenantId);
  }
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    storeRef.current.subscribe,
    storeRef.current.getSnapshot,
    storeRef.current.getServerSnapshot
  );
}

// Memoized selector keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
const selectBoundedEvents = memoize((tenantId, timeWindow) => {
  return (events) => {
    const cutoff = Date.now() - timeWindow;
    return events
      .filter(e => e.tenantId === tenantId && e.timestamp > cutoff)
      .slice(-120); // Bounded to 120 events
  };
});

// React.memo EventRow
const EventRow = memo(({ event }) => (
  <div>{event.action} - {event.timestamp}</div>
));

// Panel wiring
function AuditPanel({ tenantId }) {
  const events = useAuditStream(tenantId);
  const boundedEvents = useMemo(
    () => selectBoundedEvents(tenantId, 120000),
    [tenantId]
  )(events);

  return (
    <div>
      {boundedEvents.map(e => <EventRow key={e.id} event={e} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures, promise deduping, in-flight concurrency control, and simple caching in a realistic data-loading scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Closure-scoped internal state (inFlight, cache, queue)
- Promise coalescing for same id
- Concurrency limit with a slot-based throttle
- Time-based cache with TTL

## Code Example
```javascript
function createUserLoader() {
  const inFlight = new Map(); // id -> Promise
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> { data, expiry }
  const queue = [];
  const MAX_CONCURRENCY = 3;
  let active = 0;

  const fetchUser = async (id) => {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/user/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Network error');
    return res.json();
  };

  const tryStart = () => {
    while (active < MAX_CONCURRENCY && queue.length) {
      const { id, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      active++;
      const p = fetchUser(id)
        .then((data) => {
          cache.set(id, { data, expiry: Date.now() + 5 * 60 * 1000 });
          inFlight.delete(id);
          resolve(data);
        })
        .catch((err) => reject(err))
        .finally(() => { active--; tryStart(); });
      inFlight.set(id, p);
    }
  };

  const loadUser = (id) => {
    const cached = cache.get(id);
    if (cached && cached.expiry > Date.now()) return Promise.resolve(cached.data);
    if (inFlight.has(id)) return inFlight.get(id);
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ id, resolve, reject });
      tryStart();
    });
  };

  return { loadUser };
}

// Usage
// const loader = createUserLoader();
// loader.loadUser('123').then(console.log);
```

## Why This Is Asked
